              "text": "When a person is unable to empty their bladder completely, or at all, it is known medically as **urinary retention**. This condition is generally divided into two types:\n*   **Acute urinary retention:** A sudden, painful, and complete inability to urinate. **This is a medical emergency** that requires immediate treatment.\n*   **Chronic urinary retention:** A gradual, long-term difficulty emptying the bladder. It may cause only mild discomfort or go unnoticed at first, but it can eventually damage the bladder and kidneys if left untreated.\n\nThe causes of urinary retention typically fall into five main categories:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Blockages (Obstructions)\nA physical blockage can prevent urine from flowing out of the bladder. Common obstructions include:\n*   **Enlarged Prostate (BPH):** In men, the prostate gland surrounds the urethra (the tube that carries urine out of the body). As men age, the prostate often enlarges and squeezes the urethra, making it difficult to empty the bladder. This is the most common cause of urinary retention.\n*   **Pelvic Organ Prolapse:** In women, pelvic floor muscles can weaken, causing the bladder or uterus to sag and press against the urethra, pinching it shut.\n*   **Urethral Stricture:** Scar tissue from past surgeries, infections, or injuries can cause the urethra to narrow.\n*   **Bladder or Kidney Stones:** Stones can travel down and get stuck at the neck of the bladder, blocking the flow of urine.\n*   **Severe Constipation:** A large, hard mass of stool in the rectum can press directly against the bladder and urethra, blocking normal urine flow.\n\n### 2. Nerve and Neurological Issues\nHealthy urination requires the brain, spinal cord, and bladder nerves to communicate seamlessly. If these nerves are damaged, the brain may not get the message that the bladder is full, or the bladder muscles may fail to contract. Conditions that cause nerve-related retention include:\n*   **Spinal cord injuries or herniated discs**.\n*   **Stroke, Parkinson\u2019s disease, or Multiple Sclerosis (MS)**.\n*   **Diabetes,** which can cause diabetic neuropathy (nerve damage) affecting the bladder.\n*   **Pelvic trauma or childbirth,** which can stretch and temporarily or permanently damage the nerves in the pelvis.\n\n### 3. Medications and Anesthesia\nCertain drugs can interfere with the chemical signals that tell the bladder to squeeze, or they can cause the bladder neck to tighten up. \n*   **Anesthesia:** It is very common to experience temporary urinary retention immediately after surgery. General or spinal anesthesia numbs the nerves that control bladder function, though this usually resolves as the medication wears off.\n*   **Common Medications:** \n    *   *Antihistamines* (like allergy medications).\n    *   *Decongestants* (found in cold and flu medicines).\n    *   *Antidepressants* (especially tricyclic antidepressants).\n    *   *Muscle relaxants*.\n    *   *Opioid pain medications*.\n    *   *Overactive bladder medications* (which are designed to relax the bladder, but can sometimes over-relax it).\n\n### 4. Infection and Inflammation\nInfections and swelling can constrict the urethra or irritate the nerves surrounding the bladder:\n*   **Urinary Tract Infections (UTIs) or STIs:** These can cause the urethra to swell and become painful, making it difficult to pass urine.\n*   **Prostatitis:** Inflammation of the prostate in men can cause rapid swelling, leading to acute urinary retention.\n*   **Vaginitis:** Inflammation of the vagina in women can cause local swelling and urinary hesitancy.\n\n### 5. Weak Bladder Muscles\nAs people age, the bladder's main contracting muscle (the detrusor muscle) can weaken. If the muscle is too weak, it cannot generate enough force to squeeze all of the urine out of the bladder, leaving behind \"residual urine\".\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udea8 When to Seek Immediate Medical Attention\nYou should go to an urgent care clinic or emergency room immediately if you experience:\n*   A sudden and complete inability to urinate.\n*   Severe, sharp, or painful swelling in your lower abdomen.\n*   Fever, chills, or lower back pain alongside urinary difficulty.\n\n**Next Steps:** If you are experiencing mild or gradual difficulty emptying your bladder, you should schedule an appointment with a primary care doctor or a urologist. They can perform a quick ultrasound scan to see how much urine is left in your bladder after you pee (a post-void residual test) and help identify the exact underlying cause.",
